--expect
0 2 2 0 1 1 10 3 11 5 11 1 1 5 20 20 2 20 20
--expect-why
DERIVED FROM THE CONTRACT, not recorded from a run. Four cases, in the order
the driver prints them.

1. NOTHING COLLECTED -- `0 2 2 0`. Both scans answer false, so both functions
   return before allocating: `0` allocations. Both lists are untouched, so
   `2` and `2`. Nothing died, so `0` events.

   THIS IS THE CASE THE WHOLE PORT EXISTS FOR, and the only reading that can
   fail it is `allocs`. The shape this replaced rebuilt both lists on every
   drive iteration whether or not anything had died, and it would answer
   `2 2 0` for the other three readings exactly as this does. That waste was
   ~42 of the ~47 steps a resume cost, billed to the program on the native
   runtime and free on the other two -- so the counter is not belt and
   braces, it is the assertion.

2. ONE BRIDGE COLLECTED -- `1 1 10 3 11 5 11`. ONE allocation and no more:
   the conj for the single survivor. The fresh vector itself is free --
   `empty-vec` hands back a shared singleton -- so the count is the number of
   SURVIVORS, which is the useful thing for it to be. The dead id is NOT
   conj'd, so the list is `1` long, and the survivor kept is `10`. The host
   must hear the pair an explicit close would have produced, in that order
   and for that id: `EV_CLOSED`(3) `11`, then `EV_RELEASE`(5) `11`. Naming
   both the kind AND the id is what stops a transposed or misattributed
   event from passing.

3. A CHANNEL END COLLECTED, PEER OPEN -- `1 1 5 20 20`. Same one
   allocation, same one survivor. The peer's state becomes `P_ORPHANED`(5)
   and not `P_CLOSED`(2): a peer that vanished is not a peer that said
   goodbye, and a host that cannot tell them apart cannot report the
   difference. The waiter on the peer is failed (`20`) and the peer is woken
   (`20`) -- both, because failing a waiter that is never woken is a hang
   dressed as an error.

4. THE SAME, PEER ALREADY CLOSED -- `2 20 20`. The state stays `P_CLOSED`(2).
   An orphaning must not overwrite a close, or a port that was tidily shut
   would start reporting that its peer vanished. It is still failed and
   still woken, because a closed port can still have someone parked on it.

WHAT FAILS IF THE LOGIC IS WRONG -- RUN, not asserted. Six mutations of
`kin/reapports.kin`, each the plausible way to get this wrong, every one
caught on all three targets:

    drop the `any-collected` guard    case 1 allocs   0 -> 2
    conj the dead id too              case 2 count    1 -> 2
    emit EV_CLOSED without EV_RELEASE case 2 has no second event -- crash
    mark the peer CLOSED not ORPHANED case 3 state    5 -> 2
    write the state unconditionally   case 4 state    2 -> 5
    fail the peer but never wake it   case 3 wakes nobody -- crash

The first is why this fixture reads `allocs` at all. Its answer differs from
the correct one in THAT FIELD AND NOWHERE ELSE (`2 2 2 0` against `0 2 2 0`),
so a driver built from the lists and the events alone would have passed the
exact waste this source was written to remove. The two crashes are failures
too, just cruder ones: the driver indexes the event and woken lists, and a
missing entry takes the probe out rather than printing a wrong line.
